<!--
    LBank's mark, traced from the icon the owner supplied.

    ### Why this is traced rather than drawn

    The first version was their old blue circular mark, out of a logo archive. The second was this
    mark, but hand-fitted: circles solved from three rows each and the waist between them guessed at
    with cubics. It was close and it looked wrong — the neck too thin, the notch on the right too
    shallow — because a shape like this is not made of primitives anybody can name.

    So it is machine-traced instead. `scripts/design/trace-logo.py` scans the artwork into three
    masks, walks each boundary on the pixel grid, rounds the staircase with three rounds of Chaikin
    corner-cutting, and simplifies at 0.9px. The source is 294 pixels and this ships at 22dp — 66
    pixels at xxhdpi — so the tolerance is more than four times finer than anything that can be
    seen. Both colours are sampled from the file: #FFD900 and #161616.

    ### The three layers

    The silhouette, then the yellow field over it, then the bone. The field is traced as *yellow
    plus the bone* rather than as the yellow pixels alone: the yellow pixels form a shape with a
    hole in it, and a hole means a second contour and a fill rule to get right for no benefit.
    Painting the field whole and putting the bone back on top is one fewer thing to be wrong about.

    Never tinted. It is somebody else's mark and both colours are part of it.
-->
